+
+## Continuous receiver and quick measurement
+
+`python3 -m unittest -v` reads every current entry in `cases.json` at run time.
+It checks reproducible generation, the aligned discriminator's output, and
+complete bit recovery by the continuous receiver. The aligned discriminator is
+only expected to recover bits at nominal frequency; it does not correct offset.
+The continuous receiver knows the sample rate, symbol length and message bit
+count, but does not receive the true arrival time, frequency offset or bits. It
+searches every feasible integer-sample start and a fixed integer-Hz offset grid
+from -8 through +8 Hz, scores each candidate, and makes the bit decisions.
+This is a batch search, not a streaming or general-purpose decoder. It has no
+signal-presence detection or confidence estimate. Case changes outside that grid,
+weaker signals, different lengths or other seeds can fail; failed tests should
+be investigated rather than removed. A correct bit sequence does not imply exact
+timing or reliable recovery under noise in general.
+
+Run `python3 benchmark.py` (or `python3 benchmark.py --seconds 2` for a longer
+sample) to measure all current cases. Waveform generation and reusable
+frequency-reference preparation happen before timing, and one processing run
+warms the code. Repeated full acquisition and decision runs are timed using wall
+clock; received input duration includes the leading and trailing samples.
+Processing seconds per input second below 1 meets the serial throughput target;
+real-time margin is `(1 - processing/input) * 100%`, and the serial CPU ceiling
+is the reciprocal, in input seconds per wall second. This is a short sequential
+single-process estimate, not a measured multi-core capacity, latency guarantee,
+or allowance for other workloads. It processes the whole observation before
+returning results; generating input and preparing a new plan have a real cost
+that is excluded. Results vary with CPU load and power state.
+
+A separate `tracemalloc` run reports retained Python allocations for input and
+plan plus the peak during processing. The workspace ratio compares that peak to
+available physical pages or, when available, cgroup-v2 memory headroom, whichever
+is smaller, sampled before preparation. This is only a rough snapshot, not a
+safe concurrency limit: it excludes interpreter baseline, native allocations,
+other processes and OS overhead, and the OS or container may have other limits.
+Frequency references are reusable only for the same observation shape and
+search grid; each simultaneous independent plan and processing workspace consumes
+memory. For portable performance comparisons, preserve the case list and report
+measurement conditions and limitations along with the output.
